:16,"t":"single_choice","p":"Write the recurring decimal $0.\\dot{7}$ as a fraction in its simplest form.\n\n[Figure: table]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write the recurring decimal "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0.\\dot{7}"}},{"type":"text","text":" as a fraction in its simplest form."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["P","Q","R","S","T","U"],"rows":[["$\\dfrac{7}{9}$","$\\sqrt{1.44}$","$4^{-1} + 2^{-2}$","$\\dfrac{7}{10}$","$\\dfrac{l_L + l_U}{2}$","$1 - 3$"]],"version":1}}}]},"e":"Let $x = 0.\\dot{7}$; then $10x - x = 7 \\Rightarrow 9x = 7 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{7}{9}$. Note that $\\frac{7}{10} = 0.7$ terminates.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Number representations","st":"matching a value to its expression","cfg":{"question_number":16,"paper_question_key":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"Evaluate $4^{-1} + 2^{-2}$.\n\n[Figure: table]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4^{-1} + 2^{-2}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["P","Q","R","S","T","U"],"rows":[["$\\dfrac{7}{9}$","$\\sqrt{1.44}$","$4^{-1} + 2^{-2}$","$\\dfrac{7}{10}$","$\\dfrac{l_L + l_U}{2}$","$1 - 3$"]],"version":1}}}]},"e":"$4^{-1} + 2^{-2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$. (Note $1 - 3 = -2$.)","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Number representations","st":"matching a value to its expression","cfg":{"question_number":17,"paper_question_key":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which of the following is a rational number equal to 1.2?\n\n[Figure: table]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which of the following is a rational number equal to 1.2?"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["P","Q","R","S","T","U"],"rows":[["$\\dfrac{7}{9}$","$\\sqrt{1.44}$","$4^{-1} + 2^{-2}$","$\\dfrac{7}{10}$","$\\dfrac{l_L + l_U}{2}$","$1 - 3$"]],"version":1}}}]},"e":"$\\sqrt{1.44} = \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{100}} = \\frac{12}{10} = \\frac{6}{5} = 1.2$.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Number representations","st":"matching a value to its expression","cfg":{"question_number":18,"paper_question_key":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":19,"t":"short_answer","p":"A data centre stores $7.4 \\times 10^{11}$ bytes on one server and $4.6 \\times 10^{11}$ bytes on another. Find the total, in standard form (bytes).","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data centre stores "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"7.4 \\times 10^{11}"}},{"type":"text","text":" bytes on one server and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4.6 \\times 10^{11}"}},{"type":"text","text":" bytes on another. Find the total, in standard form (bytes)."}]}]},"e":"$(7.4 + 4.6) \\times 10^{11} = 12.0 \\times 10^{11} = 1.2 \\times 10^{12}$ bytes (also accept $1.20 \\times 10^{12}$; do not credit $12 \\times 10^{11}$).","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Scientific notation","st":"addition in scientific notation","cfg":{"question_number":19,"paper_question_key":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":1200000000000.0,"tolerance":0,"source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":20,"t":"short_answer","p":"A 6.5 m ladder leans against a wall with its foot 2.5 m from the base of the wall. Find the height, in metres, that the ladder reaches.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A 6.5 m ladder leans against a wall with its foot 2.5 m from the base of the wall. Find the height, in metres, that the ladder reaches."}]}]},"e":"$h = \\sqrt{6.5^2 - 2.5^2} = \\sqrt{42.25 - 6.25} = \\sqrt{36} = 6$ m.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Pythagoras in context","st":"finding a height","cfg":{"question_number":20,"paper_question_key":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":6,"tolerance":0,"source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"Solve for $n$: $\\dfrac{9^{n+1}}{3^{n-2}} = 3^{11}$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solve for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\dfrac{9^{n+1}}{3^{n-2}} = 3^{11}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"Write $9^{n+1} = (3^2)^{n+1} = 3^{2n+2}$; dividing by $3^{n-2}$ gives $3^{(2n+2)-(n-2)} = 3^{n+4} = 3^{11}$, so $n + 4 = 11$ and $n = 7$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Exponential equations","st":"solving with a common base","cfg":{"question_number":21,"paper_question_key":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"The recurring decimal $0.3\\dot{6}$ is written as $\\dfrac{a}{b}$ in its lowest terms. Find $a + b$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The recurring decimal "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0.3\\dot{6}"}},{"type":"text","text":" is written as "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\dfrac{a}{b}"}},{"type":"text","text":" in its lowest terms. Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"a + b"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"Let $x = 0.3\\dot{6}$: $100x - 10x = 36.\\dot{6} - 3.\\dot{6} \\Rightarrow 90x = 33 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{33}{90} = \\frac{11}{30}$. In lowest terms $a + b = 11 + 30 = 41$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Mixed repeating decimals","st":"converting to a fraction and summing a + b","cfg":{"question_number":22,"paper_question_key":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":23,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two vertical posts PQ and SR stand on a line with PQ ∥ SR. The line PS = 10 cm, SR = 17.5 cm and the segments from the crossing point O satisfy PO : OS = 4 : 10. Find PQ.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two vertical posts PQ and SR stand on a line with PQ ∥ SR. The line PS = 10 cm, SR = 17.5 cm and the segments from the crossing point O satisfy PO : OS = 4 : 10. Find PQ."}]}]},"e":"Since $PQ \\parallel SR$, $\\triangle POQ \\sim \\triangle ROS$ (AA: alternate and vertically opposite angles). Hence $\\frac{PQ}{17.5} = \\frac{4}{10} \\Rightarrow PQ = 0.4 \\times 17.5 = 7$ cm.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Similar triangles","st":"hourglass configuration","cfg":{"question_number":23,"paper_question_key":23,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":24,"t":"single_choice","p":"From the top of a cliff 48 m high, the angle of depression of a boat is 28°. Which expression gives the horizontal distance $x$ of the boat from the foot of the cliff?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"From the top of a cliff 48 m high, the angle of depression of a boat is 28°. Which expression gives the horizontal distance "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x"}},{"type":"text","text":" of the boat from the foot of the cliff?"}]}]},"e":"By alternate interior angles, the angle of elevation from the boat is 28°. Thus $\\tan 28° = \\frac{48}{x} \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{48}{\\tan 28°}$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Angle of depression","st":"choosing the correct trigonometric expression","cfg":{"question_number":24,"paper_question_key":24,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":25,"t":"single_choice","p":"An isosceles triangle has equal sides of 17 cm and a base FG of 16 cm. Find its area.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An isosceles triangle has equal sides of 17 cm and a base FG of 16 cm. Find its area."}]}]},"e":"The altitude from E bisects the base into two 8 cm halves, so $h = \\sqrt{17^2 - 8^2} = \\sqrt{289 - 64} = \\sqrt{225} = 15$ cm, and the area is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 16 \\times 15 = 120$ cm².","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Isosceles triangle & area","st":"altitude, Pythagoras and area","cfg":{"question_number":25,"paper_question_key":25,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source_fidelity":"reconstructed_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"The MYP 3 source document is a teacher marking scheme: it prints the correct answer, mark, criterion, level and rationale but not the student question stem or the wrong options. This item is reconstructed from that key and must be re-authored against the printed student paper.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":26,"t":"open_ended","p":"Decimal-to-fraction conversion [3 marks]\n(a) Convert 0.875 to a fraction in its simplest form. [1]\n(b) Convert $0.\\dot{6}\\dot{3}$ to a fraction in its simplest form, showing the algebraic subtraction. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Decimal-to-fraction conversion [3 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Convert 0.875 to a fraction in its simplest form. [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Convert "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0.\\dot{6}\\dot{3}"}},{"type":"text","text":" to a fraction in its simplest form, showing the algebraic subtraction. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\frac{875}{1000} = \\frac{7}{8}$. (b) Let $x = 0.\\dot{6}\\dot{3}$; then $100x - x = 63 \\Rightarrow 99x = 63 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{63}{99} = \\frac{7}{11}$. Unsimplified $\\frac{875}{1000}$ or $\\frac{35}{40}$ scores 0/1 in (a); writing $\\frac{7}{11}$ straight from a calculator scores 0/2 in (b).","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Decimals into fractions","st":"terminating and recurring decimals","cfg":{"question_number":26,"paper_question_key":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Terminating decimal"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Recurring decimal"}],"response_guidance":"In (a) the fraction must be fully simplified. In (b) the answer must be obtained by algebraic subtraction, not read from a calculator.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $\\frac{875}{1000} = \\frac{7}{8}$. (b) Let $x = 0.\\dot{6}\\dot{3}$; then $100x - x = 63 \\Rightarrow 99x = 63 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{63}{99} = \\frac{7}{11}$. Unsimplified $\\frac{875}{1000}$ or $\\frac{35}{40}$ scores 0/1 in (a); writing $\\frac{7}{11}$ straight from a calculator scores 0/2 in (b).","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":27,"t":"open_ended","p":"Mixed repeating decimals [4 marks]\n(a) Prove that $1.1\\dot{6} = \\dfrac{7}{6}$. [2]\n(b) Hence evaluate $1.1\\dot{6} - 0.28$ exactly, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mixed repeating decimals [4 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Prove that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"1.1\\dot{6} = \\dfrac{7}{6}"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Hence evaluate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"1.1\\dot{6} - 0.28"}},{"type":"text","text":" exactly, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Let $x = 1.1\\dot{6}$: $10x = 11.\\dot{6}$ and $100x = 116.\\dot{6}$, so $100x - 10x = 105 \\Rightarrow 90x = 105 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{105}{90} = \\frac{7}{6}$. (b) $0.28 = \\frac{7}{25}$; with common denominator 150: $\\frac{175 - 42}{150} = \\frac{133}{150}$.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Decimals into fractions","st":"mixed repeating decimals and a difference","cfg":{"question_number":27,"paper_question_key":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Proof that 1.16 recurring = 7/6"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Exact difference"}],"response_guidance":"Show the subtraction explicitly and simplify from the resulting fraction.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Let $x = 1.1\\dot{6}$: $10x = 11.\\dot{6}$ and $100x = 116.\\dot{6}$, so $100x - 10x = 105 \\Rightarrow 90x = 105 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{105}{90} = \\frac{7}{6}$. (b) $0.28 = \\frac{7}{25}$; with common denominator 150: $\\frac{175 - 42}{150} = \\frac{133}{150}$.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":28,"t":"open_ended","p":"Laws of indices [4 marks]\n(a) Simplify $15p^{-3} \\cdot q^{6} \\div (5p^{2}q^{-1})$, writing the answer with positive indices. [2]\n(b) Simplify $(3u^{-2}v^{4})^{-2} \\times 9u^{0}$, writing the answer with positive indices. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Laws of indices [4 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Simplify "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"15p^{-3} \\cdot q^{6} \\div (5p^{2}q^{-1})"}},{"type":"text","text":", writing the answer with positive indices. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Simplify "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(3u^{-2}v^{4})^{-2} \\times 9u^{0}"}},{"type":"text","text":", writing the answer with positive indices. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $15 \\div 5 \\cdot p^{-3-2} \\cdot q^{6-(-1)} = 3p^{-5}q^{7} = \\frac{3q^{7}}{p^{5}}$. (b) $(3^{-2}u^{4}v^{-8}) \\times 9 = \\frac{1}{9}u^{4}v^{-8} \\times 9 = \\frac{u^{4}}{v^{8}}$. Leaving $3p^{-5}q^{7}$ or $u^{4}v^{-8}$ scores M1 A0.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Simplifying powers","st":"laws of indices with positive indices","cfg":{"question_number":28,"paper_question_key":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Quotient and product laws"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Power of a product and zero index"}],"response_guidance":"Give every final answer with positive indices.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $15 \\div 5 \\cdot p^{-3-2} \\cdot q^{6-(-1)} = 3p^{-5}q^{7} = \\frac{3q^{7}}{p^{5}}$. (b) $(3^{-2}u^{4}v^{-8}) \\times 9 = \\frac{1}{9}u^{4}v^{-8} \\times 9 = \\frac{u^{4}}{v^{8}}$. Leaving $3p^{-5}q^{7}$ or $u^{4}v^{-8}$ scores M1 A0.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":29,"t":"open_ended","p":"Operations in scientific notation [3 marks]\n(a) Evaluate $(5.3 \\times 10^{8}) - (6.5 \\times 10^{7})$. [1]\n(b) Evaluate $(7.4 \\times 10^{-5}) + (4.6 \\times 10^{-4})$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operations in scientific notation [3 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Evaluate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(5.3 \\times 10^{8}) - (6.5 \\times 10^{7})"}},{"type":"text","text":". [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Evaluate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(7.4 \\times 10^{-5}) + (4.6 \\times 10^{-4})"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Align to $10^{8}$: $(5.3 - 0.65) \\times 10^{8} = 4.65 \\times 10^{8}$. (b) Align to $10^{-4}$: $(0.74 + 4.6) \\times 10^{-4} = 5.34 \\times 10^{-4}$. In (b) $53.4 \\times 10^{-5}$ or $0.000534$ earns M1; A1 requires the normalised $5.34 \\times 10^{-4}$.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Scientific notation","st":"addition and subtraction with aligned powers","cfg":{"question_number":29,"paper_question_key":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Subtraction"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Addition"}],"response_guidance":"Align the powers of ten first, then normalise the result to standard form.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Align to $10^{8}$: $(5.3 - 0.65) \\times 10^{8} = 4.65 \\times 10^{8}$. (b) Align to $10^{-4}$: $(0.74 + 4.6) \\times 10^{-4} = 5.34 \\times 10^{-4}$. In (b) $53.4 \\times 10^{-5}$ or $0.000534$ earns M1; A1 requires the normalised $5.34 \\times 10^{-4}$.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":30,"t":"open_ended","p":"Right-angled quadrilateral [4 marks] In quadrilateral PQRS, $\\angle PQR = 90°$, $\\angle PSR = 90°$, PQ = 7 cm, QR = 24 cm and PS = 15 cm.\n(a) Calculate the length of the diagonal PR. [2]\n(b) Calculate the perimeter of PQRS. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Right-angled quadrilateral [4 marks] In quadrilateral PQRS, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\angle PQR = 90°"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\angle PSR = 90°"}},{"type":"text","text":", PQ = 7 cm, QR = 24 cm and PS = 15 cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the length of the diagonal PR. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Calculate the perimeter of PQRS. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $PR^2 = 7^2 + 24^2 = 49 + 576 = 625$, so $PR = 25$ cm. (b) $SR = \\sqrt{25^2 - 15^2} = \\sqrt{625 - 225} = \\sqrt{400} = 20$ cm, so the perimeter is $7 + 24 + 20 + 15 = 66$ cm. Adding the diagonal (giving 91 cm) loses A1 in (b); follow-through credit is allowed from a positive PR > 15.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Pythagoras'' theorem","st":"right-angled quadrilateral","cfg":{"question_number":30,"paper_question_key":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Length of PR"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Perimeter"}],"response_guidance":"Work in triangle PQR first, then use PR in triangle PSR. The exterior perimeter excludes the diagonal.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $PR^2 = 7^2 + 24^2 = 49 + 576 = 625$, so $PR = 25$ cm. (b) $SR = \\sqrt{25^2 - 15^2} = \\sqrt{625 - 225} = \\sqrt{400} = 20$ cm, so the perimeter is $7 + 24 + 20 + 15 = 66$ cm. Adding the diagonal (giving 91 cm) loses A1 in (b); follow-through credit is allowed from a positive PR > 15.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":31,"t":"open_ended","p":"Divided triangle [4 marks] In triangle ABC, D lies on AC with BD ⊥ AC, $\\angle BAD = 42°$, BD = 10 cm and DC = 14 cm.\n(a) Calculate the length AD. [2]\n(b) Calculate the size of angle ABC. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Divided triangle [4 marks] In triangle ABC, D lies on AC with BD ⊥ AC, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\angle BAD = 42°"}},{"type":"text","text":", BD = 10 cm and DC = 14 cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the length AD. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Calculate the size of angle ABC. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\tan 42° = \\frac{10}{AD} \\Rightarrow AD = \\frac{10}{\\tan 42°} = \\frac{10}{0.9004\\ldots} = 11.11$ cm. (b) $\\tan(\\angle DBC) = \\frac{14}{10} = 1.4 \\Rightarrow \\angle DBC = 54.5°$; in triangle ABD, $\\angle ABD = 90° - 42° = 48°$, so $\\angle ABC = 48° + 54.5° = 102.5°$.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Trigonometric ratios","st":"divided triangle","cfg":{"question_number":31,"paper_question_key":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Length AD"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Angle ABC"}],"response_guidance":"Use the tangent ratio in each right-angled triangle and give lengths to 2 d.p. and angles to 1 d.p.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $\\tan 42° = \\frac{10}{AD} \\Rightarrow AD = \\frac{10}{\\tan 42°} = \\frac{10}{0.9004\\ldots} = 11.11$ cm. (b) $\\tan(\\angle DBC) = \\frac{14}{10} = 1.4 \\Rightarrow \\angle DBC = 54.5°$; in triangle ABD, $\\angle ABD = 90° - 42° = 48°$, so $\\angle ABC = 48° + 54.5° = 102.5°$.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":32,"t":"open_ended","p":"Multiples of one-eleventh [4 marks]\n(a) Write $\\dfrac{8}{11}$ as a recurring decimal and state what the sum of the two repeating digits always is. [2]\n(b) Explain, with a justification, why $\\dfrac{n}{11}$ always gives a two-digit repeating block equal to $9n$, and hence write $3.\\dot{8}\\dot{1}$ as an improper fraction. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Multiples of one-eleventh [4 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\dfrac{8}{11}"}},{"type":"text","text":" as a recurring decimal and state what the sum of the two repeating digits always is. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Explain, with a justification, why "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\dfrac{n}{11}"}},{"type":"text","text":" always gives a two-digit repeating block equal to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"9n"}},{"type":"text","text":", and hence write "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"3.\\dot{8}\\dot{1}"}},{"type":"text","text":" as an improper fraction. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\frac{8}{11} = 0.\\dot{7}\\dot{2}$; the two repeating digits always sum to 9. (b) Scaling by 9 gives $\\frac{n}{11} = \\frac{9n}{99}$, and any two-digit integer AB divided by 99 equals $0.\\dot{A}\\dot{B}$. Since $81 = 9 \\times 9$, $0.\\dot{8}\\dot{1} = \\frac{9}{11}$ and $3.\\dot{8}\\dot{1} = 3 + \\frac{9}{11} = \\frac{42}{11}$. A mixed-number answer scores R1 A0.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Decimal patterns","st":"multiples of one-eleventh","cfg":{"question_number":32,"paper_question_key":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Decimal and digit sum"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Rule and application"}],"response_guidance":"State the repeating block, then justify the rule by linking the denominator 99 to two-digit repeating decimals.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $\\frac{8}{11} = 0.\\dot{7}\\dot{2}$; the two repeating digits always sum to 9. (b) Scaling by 9 gives $\\frac{n}{11} = \\frac{9n}{99}$, and any two-digit integer AB divided by 99 equals $0.\\dot{A}\\dot{B}$. Since $81 = 9 \\times 9$, $0.\\dot{8}\\dot{1} = \\frac{9}{11}$ and $3.\\dot{8}\\dot{1} = 3 + \\frac{9}{11} = \\frac{42}{11}$. A mixed-number answer scores R1 A0.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":33,"t":"open_ended","p":"Decreasing power sequence [4 marks] A sequence has $U_1 = \\dfrac{3^4}{3^5}$, $U_2 = \\dfrac{3^6}{3^8}$, $U_3 = \\dfrac{3^8}{3^{11}}$, …\n(a) Write $U_1$, $U_2$ and $U_3$ as single powers of 3 and describe the pattern. [2]\n(b) Find and prove the general term $U_n$, and hence find $n$ when $U_n = \\dfrac{1}{2187}$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Decreasing power sequence [4 marks] A sequence has "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_1 = \\dfrac{3^4}{3^5}"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_2 = \\dfrac{3^6}{3^8}"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_3 = \\dfrac{3^8}{3^{11}}"}},{"type":"text","text":", …"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_2"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" as single powers of 3 and describe the pattern. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Find and prove the general term "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", and hence find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"U_n = \\dfrac{1}{2187}"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $U_1 = 3^{-1}$, $U_2 = 3^{-2}$, $U_3 = 3^{-3}$: each exponent decreases by 1 (each term is divided by 3). (b) Numerator exponents are $n + 1$ and denominator exponents are $3n + 2$, so $U_n = \\frac{9^{n+1}}{3^{3n+2}} = \\frac{(3^2)^{n+1}}{3^{3n+2}} = \\frac{3^{2n+2}}{3^{3n+2}} = 3^{-n}$. Then $\\frac{1}{2187} = \\frac{1}{3^7} = 3^{-7}$, so $n = 7$.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Power patterns","st":"decreasing power sequence","cfg":{"question_number":33,"paper_question_key":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"First three terms and pattern"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"General term and solving"}],"response_guidance":"State the unsimplified fraction for the general term and reduce it algebraically to the final form.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $U_1 = 3^{-1}$, $U_2 = 3^{-2}$, $U_3 = 3^{-3}$: each exponent decreases by 1 (each term is divided by 3). (b) Numerator exponents are $n + 1$ and denominator exponents are $3n + 2$, so $U_n = \\frac{9^{n+1}}{3^{3n+2}} = \\frac{(3^2)^{n+1}}{3^{3n+2}} = \\frac{3^{2n+2}}{3^{3n+2}} = 3^{-n}$. Then $\\frac{1}{2187} = \\frac{1}{3^7} = 3^{-7}$, so $n = 7$.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":34,"t":"open_ended","p":"Expanding rectangle diagonals [4 marks] Rectangle $n$ has width $2n$ cm and height 2 cm, and $d_n$ is the length of its diagonal.\n(a) Find $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_3$ in surd form and the general formula for $d_n$. [2]\n(b) Prove that $d_n$ is irrational for every integer $n \\ge 1$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expanding rectangle diagonals [4 marks] Rectangle "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" has width "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2n"}},{"type":"text","text":" cm and height 2 cm, and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" is the length of its diagonal."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_2"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" in surd form and the general formula for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Prove that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" is irrational for every integer "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n \\ge 1"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $d_1 = \\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{2}$ cm, $d_2 = \\sqrt{4^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{5}$ cm, $d_3 = \\sqrt{6^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{10}$ cm, and $d_n = \\sqrt{(2n)^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{4n^2 + 4} = 2\\sqrt{n^2 + 1}$ cm. (b) For $d_n$ to be rational, $n^2 + 1$ must be a perfect square, but $n^2 < n^2 + 1 < (n+1)^2$ for every $n \\ge 1$, so $n^2 + 1$ lies strictly between consecutive squares and is never one.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Pythagoras & irrationality","st":"expanding rectangle diagonals","cfg":{"question_number":34,"paper_question_key":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Three diagonals and the formula"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Irrationality proof"}],"response_guidance":"Give the general formula for dn, then prove the irrationality for every n ≥ 1.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $d_1 = \\sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{2}$ cm, $d_2 = \\sqrt{4^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{5}$ cm, $d_3 = \\sqrt{6^2 + 2^2} = 2\\sqrt{10}$ cm, and $d_n = \\sqrt{(2n)^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{4n^2 + 4} = 2\\sqrt{n^2 + 1}$ cm. (b) For $d_n$ to be rational, $n^2 + 1$ must be a perfect square, but $n^2 < n^2 + 1 < (n+1)^2$ for every $n \\ge 1$, so $n^2 + 1$ lies strictly between consecutive squares and is never one.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":35,"t":"open_ended","p":"Fundamental trigonometric relationship [3 marks]\n(a) Given $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{8}{17}$ and $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{15}{17}$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ and $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta$. [1]\n(b) Prove that $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$ for any right-angled triangle, and hence find $\\cos\\theta$ when $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ and $\\theta$ is acute. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fundamental trigonometric relationship [3 marks]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Given "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{8}{17}"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{15}{17}"}},{"type":"text","text":", evaluate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta"}},{"type":"text","text":". [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Prove that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1"}},{"type":"text","text":" for any right-angled triangle, and hence find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\cos\\theta"}},{"type":"text","text":" when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\theta"}},{"type":"text","text":" is acute. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\frac{8/17}{15/17} = \\frac{8}{15}$ and $\\frac{64 + 225}{289} = \\frac{289}{289} = 1$. (b) $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = (\\frac{a}{c})^2 + (\\frac{b}{c})^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2}{c^2} = \\frac{c^2}{c^2} = 1$. With $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$: $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\frac{9}{25} = \\frac{16}{25}$, and since $\\theta$ is acute $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5}$. Writing $\\pm\\frac{4}{5}$ loses A1.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Trigonometric identities","st":"the identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1","cfg":{"question_number":35,"paper_question_key":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Ratio and the identity"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Proof and application"}],"response_guidance":"Show both required results in (a), then prove the identity from Pythagoras and apply it.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $\\frac{8/17}{15/17} = \\frac{8}{15}$ and $\\frac{64 + 225}{289} = \\frac{289}{289} = 1$. (b) $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = (\\frac{a}{c})^2 + (\\frac{b}{c})^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2}{c^2} = \\frac{c^2}{c^2} = 1$. With $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$: $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\frac{9}{25} = \\frac{16}{25}$, and since $\\theta$ is acute $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5}$. Writing $\\pm\\frac{4}{5}$ loses A1.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":36,"t":"open_ended","p":"Real number subsets & counterexamples [3 marks] $T = \\{\\sqrt{81},\\ -\\sqrt{20},\\ \\dfrac{7}{12},\\ -1.\\dot{2}\\dot{3},\\ \\pi + 2,\\ 0.10110111\\ldots\\}$\n(a) Sort the six elements of $T$ into rational numbers ($\\mathbb{Q}$) and irrational numbers ($\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\mathbb{Q}$). [2]\n(b) Chantal claims that any number written with a radical sign is irrational. Show, using an element of $T$, that her claim is false. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Real number subsets & counterexamples [3 marks] "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T = \\{\\sqrt{81},\\ -\\sqrt{20},\\ \\dfrac{7}{12},\\ -1.\\dot{2}\\dot{3},\\ \\pi + 2,\\ 0.10110111\\ldots\\}"}}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Sort the six elements of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T"}},{"type":"text","text":" into rational numbers ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mathbb{Q}"}},{"type":"text","text":") and irrational numbers ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\mathbb{Q}"}},{"type":"text","text":"). [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Chantal claims that any number written with a radical sign is irrational. Show, using an element of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T"}},{"type":"text","text":", that her claim is false. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Rational: $\\sqrt{81} = 9$, $\\frac{7}{12}$, $-1.\\dot{2}\\dot{3} = -\\frac{122}{99}$. Irrational: $-\\sqrt{20} = -2\\sqrt{5}$, $\\pi + 2$, $0.10110111\\ldots$ (b) $\\sqrt{81} = 9 = \\frac{9}{1}$ is a ratio of integers, so it is rational even though it uses a radical sign.","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Rational & irrational numbers","st":"classifying numbers and a counterexample","cfg":{"question_number":36,"paper_question_key":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Classification table"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Counterexample"}],"response_guidance":"Present the classification as a clear two-column table, then answer (b) in sentences citing the integer-ratio definition.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Rational: $\\sqrt{81} = 9$, $\\frac{7}{12}$, $-1.\\dot{2}\\dot{3} = -\\frac{122}{99}$. Irrational: $-\\sqrt{20} = -2\\sqrt{5}$, $\\pi + 2$, $0.10110111\\ldots$ (b) $\\sqrt{81} = 9 = \\frac{9}{1}$ is a ratio of integers, so it is rational even though it uses a radical sign.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":37,"t":"open_ended","p":"Critiquing scientific notation working [3 marks] Sènan evaluates $(4.2 \\times 10^{-3}) - (7.0 \\times 10^{-4})$. He rewrites $7.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ as $0.07 \\times 10^{-3}$, obtains $4.13 \\times 10^{-3}$ and writes the answer as $41.3 \\times 10^{-4}$.\n(a) Identify and explain two errors in Sènan''s working. [2]\n(b) Evaluate the expression correctly. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Critiquing scientific notation working [3 marks] Sènan evaluates "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(4.2 \\times 10^{-3}) - (7.0 \\times 10^{-4})"}},{"type":"text","text":". He rewrites "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"7.0 \\times 10^{-4}"}},{"type":"text","text":" as "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0.07 \\times 10^{-3}"}},{"type":"text","text":", obtains "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4.13 \\times 10^{-3}"}},{"type":"text","text":" and writes the answer as "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"41.3 \\times 10^{-4}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Identify and explain two errors in Sènan''s working. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Evaluate the expression correctly. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Error 1 — power-of-ten alignment: $7.0 \\times 10^{-4} = 0.7 \\times 10^{-3}$, not $0.07 \\times 10^{-3}$ (he shifted two decimal places instead of one). Error 2 — invalid coefficient: $41.3 \\times 10^{-4}$ has coefficient 41.3, which violates the bound $1 \\le a < 10$. (b) $(4.2 - 0.7) \\times 10^{-3} = 3.5 \\times 10^{-3}$.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Scientific notation","st":"critiquing working","cfg":{"question_number":37,"paper_question_key":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Two errors"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Correct value"}],"response_guidance":"Name each error using formal vocabulary (power of ten / exponent, coefficient / mantissa), then give the correct evaluation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Error 1 — power-of-ten alignment: $7.0 \\times 10^{-4} = 0.7 \\times 10^{-3}$, not $0.07 \\times 10^{-3}$ (he shifted two decimal places instead of one). Error 2 — invalid coefficient: $41.3 \\times 10^{-4}$ has coefficient 41.3, which violates the bound $1 \\le a < 10$. (b) $(4.2 - 0.7) \\times 10^{-3} = 3.5 \\times 10^{-3}$.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":38,"t":"open_ended","p":"Shared-hypotenuse congruence [4 marks] In quadrilateral EFGH, $\\angle EFG = \\angle EHG = 90°$ and EF = EH = 7.5 cm. The diagonal EG is drawn.\n(a) Prove that triangle EFG is congruent to triangle EHG. [3]\n(b) Hence explain why diagonal EG bisects angle FEH. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared-hypotenuse congruence [4 marks] In quadrilateral EFGH, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\angle EFG = \\angle EHG = 90°"}},{"type":"text","text":" and EF = EH = 7.5 cm. The diagonal EG is drawn."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Prove that triangle EFG is congruent to triangle EHG. [3]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Hence explain why diagonal EG bisects angle FEH. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) In $\\triangle EFG$ and $\\triangle EHG$: $\\angle EFG = \\angle EHG = 90°$ (given); $EG = EG$ (common hypotenuse); $EF = EH = 7.5$ cm (given). Hence the triangles are congruent by RHS. (b) Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are equal, so $\\angle FEG = \\angle HEG$ and EG bisects $\\angle FEH$. Writing “SSA” or “ASS” instead of RHS loses the final accuracy mark; a Pythagoras + SSS or SAS route earns full marks.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Congruent triangles","st":"shared hypotenuse and RHS","cfg":{"question_number":38,"paper_question_key":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":3,"focus":"Congruence proof"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Bisector conclusion"}],"response_guidance":"Set out the three facts in order, name the congruence test, then draw the conclusion about the angle bisector.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) In $\\triangle EFG$ and $\\triangle EHG$: $\\angle EFG = \\angle EHG = 90°$ (given); $EG = EG$ (common hypotenuse); $EF = EH = 7.5$ cm (given). Hence the triangles are congruent by RHS. (b) Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are equal, so $\\angle FEG = \\angle HEG$ and EG bisects $\\angle FEH$. Writing “SSA” or “ASS” instead of RHS loses the final accuracy mark; a Pythagoras + SSS or SAS route earns full marks.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":39,"t":"open_ended","p":"Intersecting right triangles [4 marks] AB and ED are both perpendicular to BD, and lines AE and BD intersect at C. AB = 6 cm, BC = 8 cm and CD = 12 cm.\n(a) Prove that triangle ABC is similar to triangle EDC. [2]\n(b) Calculate the length ED and the length CE. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intersecting right triangles [4 marks] AB and ED are both perpendicular to BD, and lines AE and BD intersect at C. AB = 6 cm, BC = 8 cm and CD = 12 cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Prove that triangle ABC is similar to triangle EDC. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Calculate the length ED and the length CE. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $\\angle ABC = \\angle EDC = 90°$ (both perpendicular to BD) and $\\angle ACB = \\angle ECD$ (vertically opposite), so the triangles are similar by AA. (b) Scale factor $k = \\frac{CD}{BC} = \\frac{12}{8} = 1.5$, so $ED = 1.5 \\times 6 = 9$ cm. In right triangle ABC, $AC = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = 10$ cm, hence $CE = 1.5 \\times 10 = 15$ cm.","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Similar triangles","st":"intersecting right triangles","cfg":{"question_number":39,"paper_question_key":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Similarity proof"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Two lengths"}],"response_guidance":"Justify the similarity with two angle facts, then use the scale factor for both lengths.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $\\angle ABC = \\angle EDC = 90°$ (both perpendicular to BD) and $\\angle ACB = \\angle ECD$ (vertically opposite), so the triangles are similar by AA. (b) Scale factor $k = \\frac{CD}{BC} = \\frac{12}{8} = 1.5$, so $ED = 1.5 \\times 6 = 9$ cm. In right triangle ABC, $AC = \\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = 10$ cm, hence $CE = 1.5 \\times 10 = 15$ cm.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":40,"t":"open_ended","p":"Surd triangle classification [4 marks] Triangle LMN has LM = $\\sqrt{18}$ m, MN = $\\sqrt{32}$ m and LN = $\\sqrt{50}$ m.\n(a) Determine whether triangle LMN is right-angled, justifying your answer and naming the right angle. [2]\n(b) Find the exact perimeter of LMN and explain why it is irrational. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Surd triangle classification [4 marks] Triangle LMN has LM = "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sqrt{18}"}},{"type":"text","text":" m, MN = "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sqrt{32}"}},{"type":"text","text":" m and LN = "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sqrt{50}"}},{"type":"text","text":" m."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Determine whether triangle LMN is right-angled, justifying your answer and naming the right angle. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Find the exact perimeter of LMN and explain why it is irrational. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $LM^2 + MN^2 = 18 + 32 = 50 = LN^2$, so by the Converse of Pythagoras'' Theorem the triangle is right-angled with $\\angle LMN = 90°$ (opposite the longest side LN). (b) $LM = 3\\sqrt{2}$, $MN = 4\\sqrt{2}$, $LN = 5\\sqrt{2}$, so the perimeter is $12\\sqrt{2}$ m; since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and 12 is a non-zero rational, the product is irrational. A decimal perimeter of 16.97 m without the exact value scores 0/1.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Surds & converse of Pythagoras","st":"classifying a surd triangle","cfg":{"question_number":40,"paper_question_key":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Right-angle test"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Exact perimeter and irrationality"}],"response_guidance":"Square the sides and name the theorem you use; give the perimeter as an exact surd, not a decimal.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $LM^2 + MN^2 = 18 + 32 = 50 = LN^2$, so by the Converse of Pythagoras'' Theorem the triangle is right-angled with $\\angle LMN = 90°$ (opposite the longest side LN). (b) $LM = 3\\sqrt{2}$, $MN = 4\\sqrt{2}$, $LN = 5\\sqrt{2}$, so the perimeter is $12\\sqrt{2}$ m; since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and 12 is a non-zero rational, the product is irrational. A decimal perimeter of 16.97 m without the exact value scores 0/1.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":41,"t":"open_ended","p":"Port of Cotonou cargo [3 marks] Over 30 days the port receives $1.18 \\times 10^9$ kg of cargo and ships out $2.9 \\times 10^8$ kg, while $6.7 \\times 10^8$ kg arrives by a second terminal.\n(a) Calculate the net mass of cargo handled, in standard form. [2]\n(b) A fleet can move $4.0 \\times 10^4$ kg per trip. Determine whether 38 000 trips are enough, justifying your answer with figures. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Port of Cotonou cargo [3 marks] Over 30 days the port receives "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"1.18 \\times 10^9"}},{"type":"text","text":" kg of cargo and ships out "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2.9 \\times 10^8"}},{"type":"text","text":" kg, while "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"6.7 \\times 10^8"}},{"type":"text","text":" kg arrives by a second terminal."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the net mass of cargo handled, in standard form. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) A fleet can move "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4.0 \\times 10^4"}},{"type":"text","text":" kg per trip. Determine whether 38 000 trips are enough, justifying your answer with figures. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Align to $10^9$: $(1.18 + 0.67 - 0.29) \\times 10^9 = 1.56 \\times 10^9$ kg. (b) $38\\,000$ trips move $(3.8 \\times 10^4)(4.0 \\times 10^4) = 1.52 \\times 10^9$ kg, which is less than $1.56 \\times 10^9$ kg (39 000 trips are needed), so 38 000 trips do not suffice — short by $4.0 \\times 10^7$ kg. Saying “No” without the comparison scores 0/1.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Scientific notation in context","st":"port of Cotonou cargo","cfg":{"question_number":41,"paper_question_key":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Net mass"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Trip comparison"}],"response_guidance":"Align all masses to one power of ten, then justify the comparison with figures.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Align to $10^9$: $(1.18 + 0.67 - 0.29) \\times 10^9 = 1.56 \\times 10^9$ kg. (b) $38\\,000$ trips move $(3.8 \\times 10^4)(4.0 \\times 10^4) = 1.52 \\times 10^9$ kg, which is less than $1.56 \\times 10^9$ kg (39 000 trips are needed), so 38 000 trips do not suffice — short by $4.0 \\times 10^7$ kg. Saying “No” without the comparison scores 0/1.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":42,"t":"open_ended","p":"School media archive [3 marks] The archive holds 5 folders, each containing $2^4$ sub-folders, and each sub-folder holds $26 \\times 10^3$ KB of video.\n(a) Find the total size of the archive as an exact integer and in scientific notation (KB). [2]\n(b) The archive is then compressed to $2^{-2}$ of its size. Find the compressed size in KB. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"School media archive [3 marks] The archive holds 5 folders, each containing "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2^4"}},{"type":"text","text":" sub-folders, and each sub-folder holds "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"26 \\times 10^3"}},{"type":"text","text":" KB of video."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the total size of the archive as an exact integer and in scientific notation (KB). [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) The archive is then compressed to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2^{-2}"}},{"type":"text","text":" of its size. Find the compressed size in KB. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) $(5 \\times 2^4)(26 \\times 10^3) = 5 \\times 2^{10} \\times 10^3 = 5 \\times 1\\,024 \\times 1\\,000 = 5\\,120\\,000$ KB $= 5.12 \\times 10^6$ KB. (b) $5 \\times 2^{10} \\times 10^3 \\times 2^{-2} = 5 \\times 2^8 \\times 10^3 = 1\\,280\\,000$ KB $= 1.28 \\times 10^6$ KB.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Index laws & scientific notation","st":"school media archive","cfg":{"question_number":42,"paper_question_key":42,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Total size"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Compressed size"}],"response_guidance":"Give both the exact integer and the standard-form answer in (a).","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) $(5 \\times 2^4)(26 \\times 10^3) = 5 \\times 2^{10} \\times 10^3 = 5 \\times 1\\,024 \\times 1\\,000 = 5\\,120\\,000$ KB $= 5.12 \\times 10^6$ KB. (b) $5 \\times 2^{10} \\times 10^3 \\times 2^{-2} = 5 \\times 2^8 \\times 10^3 = 1\\,280\\,000$ KB $= 1.28 \\times 10^6$ KB.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":43,"t":"open_ended","p":"Baobab shadow survey [3 marks] A 1.75 m pole casts a shadow of 2.4 m. At the same time a baobab casts a shadow of 16.8 m.\n(a) Calculate the height of the baobab. [2]\n(b) State one physical reason why the answer is only an estimate. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Baobab shadow survey [3 marks] A 1.75 m pole casts a shadow of 2.4 m. At the same time a baobab casts a shadow of 16.8 m."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the height of the baobab. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) State one physical reason why the answer is only an estimate. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) The tree and the pole are both vertical and share the angle at the shadow tip, so the triangles are similar by AA: $\\frac{h}{1.75} = \\frac{16.8}{2.4} = 7 \\Rightarrow h = 7 \\times 1.75 = 12.25$ m. (b) Any one sensible limitation, for example: a baobab''s wide canopy means the highest shadow-casting branch is offset horizontally from the trunk centre; the ground may not be level; the shadow tip has a blurred penumbra; or the trunk may lean. Without the similarity justification, (a) scores M1 A0.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Similar triangles in context","st":"baobab shadow survey","cfg":{"question_number":43,"paper_question_key":43,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Height of the baobab"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Physical limitation"}],"response_guidance":"Justify the similarity before using the ratio, then give one sensible physical limitation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) The tree and the pole are both vertical and share the angle at the shadow tip, so the triangles are similar by AA: $\\frac{h}{1.75} = \\frac{16.8}{2.4} = 7 \\Rightarrow h = 7 \\times 1.75 = 12.25$ m. (b) Any one sensible limitation, for example: a baobab''s wide canopy means the highest shadow-casting branch is offset horizontally from the trunk centre; the ground may not be level; the shadow tip has a blurred penumbra; or the trunk may lean. Without the similarity justification, (a) scores M1 A0.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":44,"t":"open_ended","p":"Cross-river medical drone [3 marks] A drone takes off from a platform 32 m above the river and lands on a pad 8 m above it, on the opposite bank 70 m away.\n(a) Calculate the straight-line flight distance. [2]\n(b) The drone flies at 12.5 m/s. Calculate the flight time. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cross-river medical drone [3 marks] A drone takes off from a platform 32 m above the river and lands on a pad 8 m above it, on the opposite bank 70 m away."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the straight-line flight distance. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) The drone flies at 12.5 m/s. Calculate the flight time. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Vertical drop $= 32 - 8 = 24$ m and horizontal span $= 70$ m, so the distance is $\\sqrt{24^2 + 70^2} = \\sqrt{576 + 4\\,900} = \\sqrt{5\\,476} = 74$ m. (b) $\\frac{74}{12.5} = 5.92$ s.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Pythagoras in context","st":"cross-river medical drone","cfg":{"question_number":44,"paper_question_key":44,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Flight distance"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Flight time"}],"response_guidance":"Find the vertical drop and the horizontal span first, then the distance and the time.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) Vertical drop $= 32 - 8 = 24$ m and horizontal span $= 70$ m, so the distance is $\\sqrt{24^2 + 70^2} = \\sqrt{576 + 4\\,900} = \\sqrt{5\\,476} = 74$ m. (b) $\\frac{74}{12.5} = 5.92$ s.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":45,"t":"open_ended","p":"Rooftop solar panel tilt [3 marks] A solar panel of length 1.80 m is tilted so that its raised end is 0.42 m above its lower end, and it covers a horizontal width $w$.\n(a) Calculate the angle of tilt $\\theta$ and state whether the installation meets the target range $12.0° \\le \\theta \\le 15.0°$. [2]\n(b) Calculate the horizontal width $w$ covered by the panel. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rooftop solar panel tilt [3 marks] A solar panel of length 1.80 m is tilted so that its raised end is 0.42 m above its lower end, and it covers a horizontal width "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"w"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the angle of tilt "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\theta"}},{"type":"text","text":" and state whether the installation meets the target range "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"12.0° \\le \\theta \\le 15.0°"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Calculate the horizontal width "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"w"}},{"type":"text","text":" covered by the panel. [1]"}]}]},"e":"(a) The 1.80 m panel is the hypotenuse and 0.42 m is opposite $\\theta$, so $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{0.42}{1.80} = \\frac{7}{30} = 0.2333\\ldots$ and $\\theta = \\sin^{-1}(0.42 \\div 1.80) = 13.493° = 13.5°$ (1 d.p.). Since $12.0° \\le 13.5° \\le 15.0°$, the installation meets the target. (b) By Pythagoras, $w = \\sqrt{1.80^2 - 0.42^2} = \\sqrt{3.24 - 0.1764} = \\sqrt{3.0636} = 1.75$ m (2 d.p.). Using $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{0.42}{1.80}$ misidentifies the tilted panel as the horizontal base and scores 0/2 in (a); both 13.5° and the affirmative compliance statement are needed for the accuracy mark.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Trigonometry & Pythagoras in engineering","st":"rooftop solar panel tilt","cfg":{"question_number":45,"paper_question_key":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Angle of tilt and compliance"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Horizontal width"}],"response_guidance":"The tilted panel is the hypotenuse, not the horizontal base. Give the angle to 1 d.p. together with the compliance statement, and the width to 2 d.p.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","geometry","table"],"source_fidelity":"authored_from_marking_scheme","source_fidelity_note":"Authored from the official marking scheme''s printed data, subparts, mark codes, criterion and level. The scheme''s worked solution is stored as the marking guidance for this item.","marking_guidance":"(a) The 1.80 m panel is the hypotenuse and 0.42 m is opposite $\\theta$, so $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{0.42}{1.80} = \\frac{7}{30} = 0.2333\\ldots$ and $\\theta = \\sin^{-1}(0.42 \\div 1.80) = 13.493° = 13.5°$ (1 d.p.). Since $12.0° \\le 13.5° \\le 15.0°$, the installation meets the target. (b) By Pythagoras, $w = \\sqrt{1.80^2 - 0.42^2} = \\sqrt{3.24 - 0.1764} = \\sqrt{3.0636} = 1.75$ m (2 d.p.). Using $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{0.42}{1.80}$ misidentifies the tilted panel as the horizontal base and scores 0/2 in (a); both 13.5° and the affirmative compliance statement are needed for the accuracy mark.","source":"myp3-term1-summative-b-2026"}}]'::jsonb) as x
